/* Auto-generated by Whisper alignment for audio.mp3 (v4, 2848.1s). */
window.SENT = [
  { t: 0.0, text: "Every living person is the survivor of an unbroken line." },
  { t: 5.06, text: "Behind each of us stands a structure of ancestors that doubles with every step into the past." },
  { t: 12.56, text: "Two, then four, then eight, branching backward until the numbers climb into the thousands." },
  { t: 20.16, text: "Almost all of them are lost." },
  { t: 22.88, text: "Their names have gone." },
  { t: 24.58, text: "Their faces were never recorded." },
  { t: 27.06, text: "The line held, generation after generation, but it held in silence." },
  { t: 33.44, text: "Now and then, a few of those branches can still be followed." },
  { t: 37.98, text: "A parish register survives a fire." },
  { t: 40.72, text: "A soldier's nickname outlives the family that carried it." },
  { t: 44.74, text: "A date, written down by a priest at five o'clock in the morning, waits three hundred years to be read again." },
  { t: 53.0, text: "This is one such tracing." },
  { t: 56.1, text: "Four lines of descent, followed back across four countries and more than four centuries, to the farthest point the records still reach." },
  { t: 67.1, text: "It begins in 1604, in a village in the north of France, with a child carried into a stone church to be baptised." },
  { t: 75.54, text: "From there it runs through the founding of a colony, a crossing of the Atlantic under sail, a continent changing hands between empires, and the draining of a whole people southward to the mills of New England." },
  { t: 90.18, text: "And then, in a single valley in the hills of western Massachusetts, four separate families, strangers to one another for four hundred years, become one." },
  { t: 102.16, text: "This is what the records, traced back to their beginnings, revealed." },
  { t: 114.78, text: "In 1604, France was six years past the Edict of Nantes." },
  { t: 119.9, text: "After thirty-six years of religious war, in which Catholic and Protestant Frenchmen had killed each other in the name of the same God, Henri IV had granted his Protestant subjects the right to worship openly." },
  { t: 133.7, text: "The country was tired." },
  { t: 136.48, text: "The country was rebuilding." },
  { t: 138.52, text: "And the previous year, in 1603, a French explorer named Samuel de Champlain had returned from a voyage across the Atlantic, with maps of a river he called the Saint Lawrence and the rough outline of a colony he intended to plant there." },
  { t: 154.86, text: "In that year, 1604, in the village of Saint-Crépin-Ibouvilliers, in the Oise country of Picardy in northern France, a man named Jehan Bétourné carried his newborn son to the parish church to be baptised." },
  { t: 172.42, text: "The child was named Charles Bétourné." },
  { t: 178.62, text: "Saint-Crépin-Ibouvilliers was a small farming community seventy kilometres north of Paris." },
  { t: 183.64, text: "The parish register, just nineteen years old, recorded his name in a hand that has survived four centuries." },
  { t: 192.64, text: "Picardy is a quiet northern province of small parishes and grain fields." },
  { t: 198.18, text: "It would send more than its share of men across the Atlantic." },
  { t: 202.08, text: "Charles grew up there." },
  { t: 204.7, text: "In 1608, when he was four, Champlain founded Quebec City on a high promontory above the Saint Lawrence." },
  { t: 212.22, text: "In 1610, when Charles was six, an assassin in a Paris street stabbed Henri IV in the chest, and the throne passed to the new king's nine-year-old son, Louis XIII." },
  { t: 225.62, text: "The boy was crowned at Reims by candlelight." },
  { t: 229.8, text: "Charles grew up under Louis XIII's long minority and his minister Cardinal Richelieu." },
  { t: 236.22, text: "On the thirtieth of May, 1634, he married a woman also named Marguerite Bétourné, in the same church where he had been baptised." },
  { t: 245.92, text: "Marguerite was probably his cousin." },
  { t: 249.4, text: "The Bétournés were the dominant family in the parish, and the surname circulated." },
  { t: 254.86, text: "He later moved west to the commercial city of Rouen, where he died in 1660." },
  { t: 261.26, text: "His widow Marguerite followed him in death two years later." },
  { t: 264.94, text: "Their son Adrien, born in 1635, lost both parents before he turned thirty." },
  { t: 273.12, text: "Adrien would shortly cross an ocean as a soldier, and the Bétourné name in North America descends from him through three centuries to the man who, in 1908 in a Massachusetts mill town, married Maria Lepage and fathered Daniel's grandmother." },
  { t: 290.7, text: "In the same era, a thousand kilometres east, in the city of Clermont-Ferrand, in the Auvergne region of central France, at the foot of an extinct volcano called the Puy-de-Dôme, a Catholic family named Mangot worshipped at the church of Saint-Pierre." },
  { t: 306.82, text: "The deepest of them in the records is Mathieu Mangot, born sometime in the early 1600s, who married a woman named Marguerite Blandette." },
  { t: 316.04, text: "They were carpenters." },
  { t: 317.86, text: "Their family stayed at that one parish for at least four documented generations." },
  { t: 323.66, text: "Their son Antoine Mangot was baptised at Saint-Pierre in 1643, the same year the four-year-old Louis XIV inherited the throne of France from his dying father." },
  { t: 336.82, text: "Antoine married Gabrielle Pabiot." },
  { t: 340.1, text: "Their son Gilbert Mangot, also a carpenter, was born in 1677." },
  { t: 346.42, text: "Gilbert married Anne Faure." },
  { t: 349.72, text: "And in March of 1736, Gilbert's son Gabriel Mangault was baptised there at five o'clock in the morning." },
  { t: 358.56, text: "The priest noted in the register that the child's name had been written in the margin, and that the godmother's name was illegible." },
  { t: 366.7, text: "While these French families were burying their dead, another war was being fought in central Europe." },
  { t: 373.58, text: "From 1618 to 1648 the Thirty Years' War tore the German states apart." },
  { t: 381.04, text: "The Rhineland, the rich valley of the Rhine River, was crossed and re-crossed by armies, plundered, and starved." },
  { t: 389.9, text: "By the war's end, by some estimates, a third of the German-speaking population had died of violence, famine, or plague." },
  { t: 399.98, text: "The Treaty of Westphalia in 1648 ended the fighting and redrew the map, but the Rhineland's villages were emptied." },
  { t: 408.92, text: "It would take fifty years for them to refill." },
  { t: 412.6, text: "In that recovering country, in or near the town of Baumholder, a family named Heppell lived as German Lutherans." },
  { t: 421.64, text: "Philipp Jacob Heppell and his wife Margretha raised their children there in the Lutheran faith, in a region where Catholics and Protestants now lived side by side under the new principle, set at Westphalia, that each prince determined his territory's religion." },
  { t: 440.44, text: "Their son Johann Nicolaus Heppell, who became a surgeon, was baptised on the twenty-second of July, 1707." },
  { t: 450.4, text: "Johann Nicolaus married Maria Barbara Reissen on the twenty-sixth of January, 1734, near Homburg." },
  { t: 459.18, text: "And on the Atlantic coast of France, in the old province of Saintonge, a fourth family was living in the town of Pons." },
  { t: 467.92, text: "Pons was an old Protestant centre." },
  { t: 471.36, text: "The Edict of Nantes was still in force; the temple still stood." },
  { t: 476.04, text: "But in a faubourg of Pons called Saint-Vivien, a Catholic merchant named Pierre Barbeau and his wife Madeleine Babin were raising a family through the 1660s." },
  { t: 488.64, text: "The Pons parish index records three of their children: Pierre, Magdelaine, and Marie." },
  { t: 496.04, text: "And in 1666 they had a son named Jean." },
  { t: 501.2, text: "The same year, on the other side of the ocean, French soldiers were fighting in the Mohawk valley." },
  { t: 508.12, text: "These four European families, in Picardy, in Auvergne, in the Rhineland, and in Saintonge, would all eventually send descendants to one small mill town in the Berkshire Hills of western Massachusetts." },
  { t: 523.54, text: "There their great-grandchildren would meet, marry, and produce Daniel Lepel and Renee Lepel Hanson, their cousins Jon and Christine, and many other cousins, aunts, and uncles across all four family lines." },
  { t: 539.2, text: "This is how it happened." },
  { t: 550.02, text: "The first of Daniel and Renee's documented ancestors to cross the Atlantic did so under sail with a regiment of soldiers." },
  { t: 558.22, text: "In 1661, the young Louis XIV had begun his personal reign." },
  { t: 564.86, text: "He turned his attention to New France." },
  { t: 568.32, text: "The colony, with a French population of only three thousand, had been raided for two decades by the Iroquois Confederacy and was hemorrhaging back to France." },
  { t: 578.9, text: "The King's answer was military." },
  { t: 582.0, text: "In June and July of 1665, the Carignan-Salières Regiment disembarked at Quebec City." },
  { t: 588.3, text: "Twelve hundred soldiers had been sent across the ocean by King Louis the Fourteenth." },
  { t: 593.28, text: "They were the first regular French troops the colony had ever seen." },
  { t: 619.9, text: "Among the Carignan-Salières soldiers was Adrien Bétourné." },
  { t: 625.98, text: "He was thirty years old, and he had just buried his parents in France." },
  { t: 631.52, text: "He carried a soldier's nickname, dit Laviolette, called the violet, one of the regiment's noms de guerre alongside men called Lapierre, Sansoucy, and Lafleur." },
  { t: 644.72, text: "The regiment fought two campaigns against the Mohawk villages in 1666." },
  { t: 650.66, text: "When the regiment was disbanded in 1668, eight hundred soldiers returned to France and four hundred stayed." },
  { t: 657.8, text: "Adrien Bétourné dit Laviolette was one of the four hundred." },
  { t: 664.78, text: "To root the soldiers to the colony, the Crown granted each one who stayed a seigneurial concession, a parcel of unimproved land along the Richelieu River or around Montreal, to clear and farm." },
  { t: 680.1, text: "Adrien took up his grant on the south shore of the Saint Lawrence, in the country that would become Longueuil and La Prairie." },
  { t: 687.48, text: "His descendants would live and die on that grant for the next two centuries, then carry the family name south into New York and Massachusetts under the spelling Bitournay." },
  { t: 701.8, text: "In 1669 Adrien married Marie Deshaies, possibly one of the Filles du Roi, the King's Daughters whom Louis had begun sending to the colony to balance its lopsided male population: eight hundred women, between 1663 and 1673, whose Atlantic passage the King paid for the explicit purpose of marrying the soldier-settlers." },
  { t: 702.76, text: "With that marriage, the Bétourné line had begun in North America." },
  { t: 707.86, text: "Twenty years later, a second soldier crossed." },
  { t: 712.38, text: "In 1685, the King's policy toward New France changed again." },
  { t: 718.6, text: "New companies of the troupes de la marine were shipped out to garrison the colony, sailing with the incoming governor general, the marquis de Denonville. 1685 was also the year Louis the Fourteenth revoked the Edict of Nantes, outlawing the Protestant faith in France and driving hundreds of thousands of Huguenots into exile." },
  { t: 743.16, text: "Pons, the Saintonge town where the Barbeau family lived, was one of the great Protestant centres of France." },
  { t: 751.12, text: "But the Barbeaus were Catholic, and the young man who left Pons that year left not as a refugee but as a soldier of the King." },
  { t: 761.48, text: "He was Jean Barbeau, the merchant's son, about nineteen years old." },
  { t: 768.62, text: "He came to Canada in the company of an officer called the sieur de Saint-Cirque." },
  { t: 774.34, text: "Like every soldier of his day, Jean was given a regimental nickname." },
  { t: 779.66, text: "His was Boisdoré, golden wood." },
  { t: 784.44, text: "It stuck to him, and to his children, and to theirs, for two hundred years." },
  { t: 790.78, text: "The family would sign itself Barbeau dit Boisdoré." },
  { t: 796.12, text: "Jean Barbeau stayed after his service." },
  { t: 799.76, text: "On the eighteenth of November, 1686, the year after he landed, he married Marie Françoise de Noyon at the church of Sainte-Famille in Boucherville, on the south shore of the Saint Lawrence below Montreal." },
  { t: 814.16, text: "Marie Françoise was the daughter of Jean de Noyon, a toolmaker from Normandy, and Marie Chauvin, married at Trois-Rivières in 1665." },
  { t: 826.08, text: "Jean and Marie Françoise had sixteen children." },
  { t: 830.82, text: "The line ran on through their son Gabriel Barbeau dit Boisdoré, baptised at Boucherville in 1694, who married Marie-Madeleine Lory in Montreal in 1719, and settled at La Prairie." },
  { t: 831.72, text: "Gabriel and Marie-Madeleine's son Jean-Baptiste Barbeau dit Boisdoré, baptised in 1725, married Marguerite Gagné at La Prairie in 1750." },
  { t: 861.58, text: "In 1701, fifty French and English delegates and the chiefs of thirty-nine First Nations signed the Great Peace of Montreal, the treaty that ended the long wars Adrien Bétourné had been sent to fight." },
  { t: 876.54, text: "The European wars went on." },
  { t: 878.96, text: "From 1701 to 1714 the War of the Spanish Succession set Europe and the colonies against each other again; in North America it was called Queen Anne's War." },
  { t: 890.76, text: "In 1709, in the brutal cold of Le Grand Hiver, the Great Winter, the wine froze in the cellars of Versailles and tens of thousands starved across France." },
  { t: 903.42, text: "The Treaty of Utrecht in 1713 transferred Acadia and Newfoundland from France to Britain." },
  { t: 910.3, text: "The line dividing the two empires moved closer to Quebec." },
  { t: 914.36, text: "In Auvergne, the Mangot carpenters were still in France." },
  { t: 918.84, text: "Sometime after 1736, Gabriel or his son crossed to Quebec, and the surname slowly turned from Mangault to Mongeau." },
  { t: 929.94, text: "To keep the memory of their parish, they attached a nickname, Mongeau dit Clermont, the Clermont pointing straight back to Clermont-Ferrand." },
  { t: 942.36, text: "The Mongeau crossing fell during the War of the Austrian Succession, which in North America was called King George's War, 1740 to 1748: more troops, more raids, more burned villages on both sides of the colonial frontier." },
  { t: 961.9, text: "In the Rhineland, the Heppells had crossed to British colonial America in the late 1720s, joining the wave of German Lutheran emigrants who were settling Salem County in the province of New Jersey under William Penn's old promise of religious freedom." },
  { t: 978.3, text: "Johann Nicolaus's son, John Jacob Jean Heppell, was born there on the twenty-fourth of March, 1750, and baptised at the German Lutheran church at Friesburg." },
  { t: 990.5, text: "And in Quebec, the Barbeau line had reached its fourth American generation." },
  { t: 996.34, text: "Joseph Barbeau dit Boisdoré was baptised at Saint-Constant in 1757." },
  { t: 1002.44, text: "He was born a subject of the King of France, and he married Marie Louise Bulteau at La Prairie in 1781." },
  { t: 1011.2, text: "By then everything had changed." },
  { t: 1014.32, text: "In 1755 the British colonial governor in Acadia had ordered the Acadian Expulsion: the rounding-up and deportation of the French Catholic farmers of the Bay of Fundy, the Grand Dérangement that scattered seven thousand people across the Atlantic seaboard." },
  { t: 1033.06, text: "In 1756 the Seven Years' War broke out, in North America the French and Indian War, with the French and the British contesting the future of the continent." },
  { t: 1046.38, text: "In 1759, on the Plains of Abraham above Quebec City, the British general Wolfe and the French general Montcalm fought a battle that lasted fifteen minutes and ended both their lives." },
  { t: 1061.78, text: "Quebec fell." },
  { t: 1064.02, text: "In 1763 the Treaty of Paris ceded New France to Britain." },
  { t: 1070.66, text: "Joseph Barbeau was six years old when the treaty was signed." },
  { t: 1075.42, text: "He went to sleep one night a French subject and woke the next a British one, without ever leaving his family's farm." },
  { t: 1095.68, text: "The British were unsure what to do with their new French Catholic colony." },
  { t: 1100.56, text: "In 1774 they decided." },
  { t: 1102.9, text: "The Quebec Act preserved French civil law and the Catholic religion, in defiance of every Protestant English settler's expectation." },
  { t: 1113.16, text: "It was one of the grievances cited by the American colonists who, the next year, rose in revolt." },
  { t: 1120.74, text: "In 1775 the American Revolution broke out in colonial America." },
  { t: 1126.4, text: "John Jacob Jean Heppell, by then twenty-five and living in New Jersey, watched the colony tear itself apart." },
  { t: 1134.38, text: "By 1790 he had migrated north to Quebec, settling far down the Saint Lawrence at Rimouski." },
  { t: 1142.54, text: "On the nineteenth of July, 1790, at the Cathédrale Saint-Germain in Rimouski, this Anglo-German Protestant married a French-Canadian Catholic woman, Agnès Lavoie, daughter of Joseph Lavoie and Marie Charlotte Gagnon." },
  { t: 1160.46, text: "Joseph and Marie Charlotte had themselves married on the eighteenth of January, 1745, at Les Éboulements on the north shore of the Saint Lawrence, and raised twelve children." },
  { t: 1174.28, text: "After Agnès died, John Jacob married Victoire Bouillon, daughter of Jacques Bouillon and Catherine Landois, on the twenty-fourth of April, 1798, also at Rimouski." },
  { t: 1187.58, text: "The direct Heppell line ran through Victoire, not Agnès." },
  { t: 1194.78, text: "The Heppell name began to erode at the edges." },
  { t: 1199.48, text: "French clerks could not hear the H. The surname slid from Heppell to Epelle to Hepel." },
  { t: 1208.36, text: "In the same century, on the south shore of the Saint Lawrence directly across from Quebec City, a fourth family was taking root in the parish of Saint-Joseph-de-la-Pointe-Lévy, the town known as Lauzon." },
  { t: 1224.32, text: "The Lepages of Lauzon were working people." },
  { t: 1228.36, text: "The deepest of them in the records is Joseph Marie Lepage, married to Marie Goupil sometime in the early 1700s." },
  { t: 1238.12, text: "Their son Michel Lepage married Catherine Huard on the twenty-eighth of May, 1805, at Lauzon." },
  { t: 1246.02, text: "Michel and Catherine's son, Joseph Lepage, was born at Lauzon on the first of February, 1812." },
  { t: 1254.26, text: "He grew up to be a scieur de long, a pit sawyer, the trade in which one man stood in a pit beneath a log and a second stood on top, drawing a long saw up and down between them to cut boards." },
  { t: 1269.84, text: "It was brutal work." },
  { t: 1271.9, text: "On the seventeenth of February, 1846, at Lauzon, Joseph Lepage the pit sawyer married Josephte Couture, daughter of Joseph Couture and Marie Josèphe Blais, themselves married in 1808 at the neighbouring parish of Saint-Henri-de-Lauzon." },
  { t: 1291.12, text: "Joseph the pit sawyer could not sign his own name." },
  { t: 1295.28, text: "On the twenty-seventh of March, 1847, Joseph and Josephte's first son was baptised at Lauzon." },
  { t: 1302.94, text: "He was named Joseph, after his father." },
  { t: 1307.36, text: "His godfather, Michel Lepage, was the only adult at the baptism who could write his name; the father could not, the godmother could not." },
  { t: 1318.6, text: "This second Joseph Lepage would not be a pit sawyer." },
  { t: 1322.16, text: "He would learn a skilled trade." },
  { t: 1325.04, text: "He would become a charpentier, a carpenter, the trade that requires you to read measurements and write contracts." },
  { t: 1333.78, text: "His son would carry the family one further step away from the saw pit and into the schools of New England." },
  { t: 1341.42, text: "Across the ocean, in those same years, France was tearing itself apart." },
  { t: 1348.04, text: "The French Revolution broke out in 1789." },
  { t: 1351.5, text: "Louis XVI was beheaded in 1793." },
  { t: 1355.4, text: "Napoleon Bonaparte came to power in 1799 and made himself emperor in 1804." },
  { t: 1362.54, text: "From 1803 to 1815 the Napoleonic Wars killed perhaps four million people across Europe." },
  { t: 1375.24, text: "The Bétournés, Mangots, Barbeaus, and Lepages, by now all in Quebec, watched these events from across the Atlantic; the news arrived in the colonial papers two months late." },
  { t: 1385.92, text: "Their cousins who had stayed behind in France were conscripted." },
  { t: 1390.38, text: "Napoleon was a folk hero in Quebec; even his enemies admired him." },
  { t: 1395.0, text: "While the Heppells were becoming Quebec French-Canadians, the Barbeaus were moving across the farm country south of Montreal called the Montérégie." },
  { t: 1404.46, text: "Joseph Barbeau's son François Régis Barbeau was born at Saint-Constant in 1804, the year Napoleon was crowned." },
  { t: 1413.9, text: "He married Catherine Forgues there in 1828; she was the daughter of Michel Forgues and Catherine Tougas." },
  { t: 1421.76, text: "The family carried on to Saint-Philippe de Laprairie and then toward Saint-Édouard de Napierville." },
  { t: 1428.64, text: "The Bétournés dit Laviolette, descendants of the Carignan-Salières soldier, were rooted in the same Montérégie, in Longueuil and Laprairie, having married into the Dupuis, Raymond, and Daigle families along the way." },
  { t: 1443.34, text: "In 1837, in those same Montérégie villages, the Patriotes rose against the British colonial government in the Lower Canada Rebellion." },
  { t: 1453.36, text: "The rebellion was crushed within months and the leaders hanged or exiled, but it set in motion the political reforms that, decade by decade, would eventually produce a Canadian self-government." },
  { t: 1467.32, text: "By the early 1800s the old nicknames were fading." },
  { t: 1472.14, text: "The Barbeau dit Boisdorés simply signed Barbeau." },
  { t: 1472.14, text: "The Bétournés dit Laviolettes simply signed Bétourné." },
  { t: 1480.58, text: "The Mongeau dit Clermont family did the opposite of its neighbours." },
  { t: 1485.32, text: "Instead of dropping the nickname, they dropped the original surname and kept Clermont, the place-name that pointed back to Auvergne." },
  { t: 1504.52, text: "By 1850, the four ancestral lines were converging on two separate parts of Quebec." },
  { t: 1511.88, text: "Three would settle into Saint-Édouard de Napierville in the Montérégie." },
  { t: 1516.92, text: "The fourth, the Lepages, were already at Lévis on the south shore of the Saint Lawrence, and would shortly add a fifth thread when a young woman from a Bellechasse parish married into them." },
  { t: 1528.8, text: "In a small village in the seigneurie of Bellechasse, on the south shore eighteen kilometres east of Lévis, a man named Laurent Noël lived as a cabotteur, a coastal boatman who sailed the short-haul routes of the lower Saint Lawrence." },
  { t: 1545.54, text: "He married Marguerite Duquet, of a family also known as Duquet dit Marquette." },
  { t: 1552.8, text: "Like Joseph Lepage the pit sawyer, Laurent Noël could not sign his name." },
  { t: 1559.46, text: "Their daughter, baptised Marie Virginie Noël, was born and baptised on the same day, the twenty-third of July, 1855, at the church of Saint-Michel de Bellechasse." },
  { t: 1572.48, text: "Her godfather was her uncle, Jean Duquet." },
  { t: 1576.52, text: "Marie Virginie went by her second name, Virginie." },
  { t: 1580.8, text: "By the time she was fifteen, both her parents were dead." },
  { t: 1584.96, text: "She was an orphan." },
  { t: 1586.76, text: "A male relative on her mother's side, François Marquette, became her legal guardian." },
  { t: 1592.92, text: "She was moved to Lévis, the larger town up the river, where the parish of Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire stood at the centre of the busy port community." },
  { t: 1602.94, text: "On the twenty-first of January, 1873, at Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire in Lévis, Virginie Noël married Joseph Lepage the carpenter, the second-generation Lepage of Lauzon." },
  { t: 1616.22, text: "She was seventeen years old and a minor under Quebec law of the day; her guardian François Marquette signed the consent in place of her dead parents." },
  { t: 1627.9, text: "Joseph the carpenter was twenty-five." },
  { t: 1631.34, text: "The two of them would settle in the parish of her marriage." },
  { t: 1634.7, text: "Over the next twenty-three years they would have twelve children there, baptised one by one at Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire." },
  { t: 1643.28, text: "The fifth of those twelve children was a daughter, born late on the afternoon of the nineteenth of September, 1883." },
  { t: 1648.14, text: "Four days later, on the twenty-third of September, the vicar of Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire baptised her." },
  { t: 1660.76, text: "He wrote in the register: avons baptisé Marie Hélène Cléophée, née quatre jours auparavant, fille légitime de Joseph Lepage, charpentier, et de Virginie Noël, de cette paroisse." },
  { t: 1677.22, text: "Her godfather was her uncle on the Noël side, François Xavier Noël." },
  { t: 1683.6, text: "Her godmother was his wife, Marie Cléophée Pussette, after whom the child took her third given name." },
  { t: 1692.26, text: "Marie Hélène Cléophée Lepage." },
  { t: 1696.24, text: "Joseph the carpenter could not sign his name either, the priest noted; neither could the godparents." },
  { t: 1703.66, text: "Three generations of Lepage men, the pit sawyer, the carpenter, and the carpenter's children's father, had moved up the social ladder one trade at a time, but the writing had not yet caught them." },
  { t: 1718.02, text: "Marie Hélène Cléophée Lepage would later cross to Massachusetts, change her name to Maria, marry a man whose own name had crossed the border and reshaped itself, and become Daniel's great-grandmother on his father's side." },
  { t: 1735.08, text: "While the Lepages were raising their twelve children at Lévis, three other ancestral lines were converging on Saint-Édouard de Napierville, in the Montérégie south of Montreal." },
  { t: 1747.3, text: "On the twenty-seventh of November, 1865, at the Saint-Édouard de Napierville parish, Magloire Barbeau married Marie Blais, the daughter of Joseph Blais and Esther Poissant." },
  { t: 1760.0, text: "Magloire had been born at Saint-Philippe in 1833, a great-great-grandson of the soldier Jean Barbeau." },
  { t: 1767.78, text: "Magloire and Marie's son, Joseph Wilfred Barbeau, was baptised at Saint-Édouard de Napierville on the twenty-eighth of April, 1870." },
  { t: 1778.24, text: "He was a documented Canadian by birth, baptised in a Canadian parish, registered in a Canadian register, three years after the British North America Act had created the Dominion of Canada." },
  { t: 1791.68, text: "Joseph Wilfred Barbeau would, before his fortieth year, cross the border south to North Adams, Massachusetts, work in the textile mills, raise a son there, and die there in 1938." },
  { t: 1806.28, text: "His Canadian birth, recorded in 1870 in a Quebec parish register, would matter again a century and a half later." },
  { t: 1816.1, text: "In the same Saint-Édouard de Napierville parish, in the same generation, Évariste Mongeau dit Clermont married Élise Boulerisse in 1878. Élise was the daughter of Médard Boulerice and Julienne Rougeau, of farming families that had been in the Montérégie since the early 1700s." },
  { t: 1841.3, text: "A generation of Bétournés worshipped at the same church." },
  { t: 1844.6, text: "The priest knew them all." },
  { t: 1849.1, text: "Three of Daniel and Renee's four ancestral lines lived in one Quebec village in one generation, three centuries removed from Picardy, Auvergne, and Saintonge, and none of them knew their American descendants would meet again." },
  { t: 1866.5, text: "In Europe, the Rhineland was emptying out for the second time." },
  { t: 1872.78, text: "The Franco-Prussian War of 1870 ended with the French defeat at Sedan and the proclamation of the German Empire in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles." },
  { t: 1883.54, text: "The Heppell cousins who had stayed in Baumholder were now subjects of the new Kaiser." },
  { t: 1889.98, text: "They would be drafted into German wars for the next seventy-five years." },
  { t: 1895.28, text: "But then the mills called." },
  { t: 1898.54, text: "Between 1840 and 1930, roughly nine hundred thousand French-Canadians left Quebec for the textile and paper mills of New England." },
  { t: 1909.2, text: "The migration was massive, sustained, and largely forgotten outside the families that lived it." },
  { t: 1915.96, text: "Whole villages emptied." },
  { t: 1918.1, text: "Whole parishes were transplanted to mill towns south of the border." },
  { t: 1922.52, text: "Modeste Bétournay, a cousin of the family that would eventually carry the Bitournay name south, crossed the border in 1887 and settled in the cotton-mill country around Holyoke." },
  { t: 1935.8, text: "Henri C. Clermont, born at Saint-Liboire de Bagot in 1880 to Évariste and Élise, crossed south in 1897 at the age of seventeen and found work in a Berkshire County cotton mill; in 1898 at Williamstown, Massachusetts, he married Alexina Emma Rancourt, also born at Saint-Édouard de Napierville." },
  { t: 1963.12, text: "Hermenegilde Hepel, of the Rimouski Heppell line, crossed to the textile city of Fall River, Massachusetts, around 1900, and married Maria Sirois there in 1902." },
  { t: 1975.96, text: "A different Bétourné branch had already crossed earlier and settled across the border in upstate New York, in the small parish of Redford in Clinton County, just south of the Quebec border." },
  { t: 1989.2, text: "The Quebec spelling had begun to slip there." },
  { t: 1993.22, text: "English-speaking clerks heard the French é and wrote it as i; they heard the rné ending and wrote it as nay; the name Bétourné became, by 1880, the name Bitournay." },
  { t: 2009.82, text: "In Redford in 1880, a man named Joseph Bitournay and his wife Elmire Rougier had a son." },
  { t: 2019.54, text: "They named him Joseph, after his father." },
  { t: 2023.04, text: "He would grow up in New York, learn the textile trade as a weaver, and follow the mills east to Massachusetts." },
  { t: 2031.46, text: "And the Lepages were also drifting south, child by child, year by year, the twelve children of Joseph the carpenter and Virginie Noël scattering from Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire into the New England mill towns." },
  { t: 2048.36, text: "Marie Hélène Cléophée Lepage, the fifth child, born 1883, was already in North Adams by the time of her twenty-fifth birthday." },
  { t: 2059.22, text: "So was her husband-to-be." },
  { t: 2070.98, text: "By the early 1900s all four family lines were converging on one corner of New England, the Berkshire Hills of western Massachusetts, where the towns of Adams, North Adams, and Williamstown clustered along the Hoosic River and the mills employed thousands of French-Canadian workers." },
  { t: 2092.94, text: "Joseph Wilfred Barbeau, the Canadian-born from Napierville, married Marie Georgiana Trudeau in the early 1900s." },
  { t: 2100.96, text: "They had a son, Rouville Ernest Barbeau, in 1906 at North Adams." },
  { t: 2107.86, text: "Henri Clermont and Alexina Rancourt had a daughter, Regina Hermanise Clermont, in 1907 at Williamstown." },
  { t: 2117.6, text: "On the twenty-fifth of August, 1908, at Notre Dame du Sacré Coeur in North Adams, two French-Canadian threads tied themselves together in a single marriage." },
  { t: 2128.88, text: "A weaver named Joseph Bitournay, twenty-eight years old, born in Redford, New York, the son of Joseph Bitournay and Elmire Rougier, married Maria Lepage, a mill operative, who gave her age as twenty-three and her birthplace as Lévis, Canada." },
  { t: 2147.36, text: "She was actually almost twenty-five; she had been born twenty-four years and eleven months earlier as Marie Hélène Cléophée Lepage at Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire on the south shore of the Saint Lawrence, the fifth of twelve children of Joseph the carpenter and Virginie Noël the orphan from Bellechasse." },
  { t: 2168.84, text: "The priest who married them, Father C.N. Jeannotte, recorded both sets of parents on the same line of the same page of the North Adams marriage register." },
  { t: 2179.32, text: "With that one document, the Bétourné family of Picardy and the Lepage family of Lauzon were joined under one roof on Walker Street in North Adams." },
  { t: 2191.5, text: "Their daughter, Rita Marie Betourney, would be Daniel's grandmother." },
  { t: 2196.24, text: "In 1914 the First World War broke out in Europe." },
  { t: 2201.0, text: "Most French-Canadian men in the Berkshires were not eligible for the Canadian draft, but a number volunteered for the American Expeditionary Force after 1917." },
  { t: 2210.8, text: "The mills ran full out for the war effort, and ran full out again for the influenza of 1918 and 1919, in which two out of every three Berkshire mill families lost someone." },
  { t: 2224.9, text: "On the seventh of September, 1931, in the middle of the Great Depression, Rouville Ernest Barbeau married Regina Hermanise Clermont at St. Raphael's in Williamstown." },
  { t: 2237.86, text: "The Barbeau and Clermont lines became one." },
  { t: 2242.66, text: "In 1943, in North Adams, they had a daughter, Lorraine Irene Barbeau." },
  { t: 2249.26, text: "Lorraine is Daniel and Renee's mother." },
  { t: 2253.58, text: "She was the youngest of six children: five older brothers, Robert, William, Bernard, Richard, and Francis, the last of whom went by Eddie." },
  { t: 2263.78, text: "A couple of those brothers served in the United States military." },
  { t: 2268.64, text: "On the paternal side, Hermenegilde Hepel and Maria Sirois's son, Alcide J. Lepel, was born in Fall River in 1911 and moved west to North Adams as a young man." },
  { t: 2281.02, text: "In 1935, at North Adams, Alcide married Rita Marie Betourney, daughter of Joseph Bitournay the weaver and Maria Lepage the mill operative." },
  { t: 2293.46, text: "Their son, Ronald Joseph Lepel, was born in 1939 at North Adams, the year the Second World War broke out." },
  { t: 2302.52, text: "As a boy, Ronald was sent to boarding school in Canada; he spoke Québécois French before he spoke English." },
  { t: 2310.56, text: "The Second World War took Berkshire boys to the South Pacific, to North Africa, to Normandy, and to the Rhineland, the same valley the Heppells had left in the 1720s, now the staging ground for the Allied push into Germany." },
  { t: 2328.44, text: "The mills produced cloth for uniforms and parachutes." },
  { t: 2332.64, text: "The veterans came back to the same towns, the same parishes, the same priests." },
  { t: 2339.58, text: "In Korea, between 1950 and 1953, the United States fought to a stalemate at the same parallel where the war had begun." },
  { t: 2350.0, text: "Lorraine's eldest brother, Bernard Barbeau, served there as a Sergeant in the U.S. Army, a medic with the Forty-fifth Infantry Division, before returning to the Adams and North Adams area." },
  { t: 2365.12, text: "By then the textile mills that had drawn the family south were closing one by one." },
  { t: 2370.94, text: "Sprague Electric, founded by Robert Sprague in 1926 and headquartered in the old Marshall Street mill complex along the Hoosic, had taken their place as North Adams' dominant employer." },
  { t: 2385.62, text: "Several of the family worked there: Bernard on the manufacturing line for over forty years, Paul Lepel as a computer programmer for twenty-three years, Ronald himself for seven years before deciding to go into business for himself." },
  { t: 2403.6, text: "In 1966 he purchased the Boston Fish Market from the Battersby family, who had founded it nearly thirty years before; the sale included the Battersbys' authentic recipe for battered cod that would become the restaurant's signature fish and chips." },
  { t: 2421.76, text: "He moved the market to a former tavern on West Main Street, a couple of blocks from the Sprague Electric gate." },
  { t: 2429.6, text: "He and Lorraine ran the business, later renamed the Boston Seafood Restaurant and Caterers, for thirty-eight years before retiring to Sun City West, Arizona." },
  { t: 2442.1, text: "Lorraine, who graduated from the Bishop Memorial Training School for Nurses at Pittsfield in 1964 and joined the staff at North Adams Hospital that same year, would later serve as Robert Sprague's private duty nurse in the years leading up to his death in 1991." },
  { t: 2462.82, text: "Sprague itself closed in 1985, taking with it the industrial work that had supported four generations of Berkshire French-Canadians." },
  { t: 2473.84, text: "Ronald Joseph Lepel married Lorraine Irene Barbeau." },
  { t: 2479.62, text: "With that single marriage the four lines became one." },
  { t: 2485.6, text: "The Picardy soldier, the Auvergne carpenters, the Rhineland Lutherans, and the Saintonge merchant's son who crossed as a soldier of the King, all converged in one household in North Adams, Massachusetts." },
  { t: 2500.12, text: "Ronald and Lorraine had two children, Daniel Lepel and Renee Lepel Hanson." },
  { t: 2506.56, text: "They grew up in North Adams and were baptised at Notre Dame du Sacré Coeur, the French-Canadian parish where four generations of the family had worshipped." },
  { t: 2516.56, text: "When Daniel was eight, the family moved across the Hoosic to Williamstown, to the same parish, St. Raphael's, where Rouville and Regina had married thirty-four years earlier." },
  { t: 2528.41, text: "They learned to say the surname the French way, luh-PELL, though to this day people unfamiliar with it say LEP-uhl, and some, spelling the name the way they hear it, write it with an a, Lapel." },
  { t: 2542.78, text: "The Clermont name on their mother's side had Americanized too, from the French clair-MOHN to a hard CLAIR-mont." },
  { t: 2551.38, text: "In adulthood Daniel moved to Albany, New York; his cousin Jon Lepel lives in the same state." },
  { t: 2561.92, text: "On the fifteenth of December, 2025, Bill C-3 took effect in Canada." },
  { t: 2568.66, text: "The law restored citizenship by descent past the first generation, with a single requirement: at least one Canadian-born ancestor in the direct line had to have been alive past 1947, the year the Canadian Citizenship Act first defined what a Canadian citizen was." },
  { t: 2589.36, text: "The research that followed reached back four hundred and twenty-two years." },
  { t: 2595.0, text: "It found a boy baptised in Picardy in 1604, the year Champlain returned from his second voyage." },
  { t: 2602.72, text: "It found the Mangot carpenters of Clermont-Ferrand, working in stone and oak under five French kings." },
  { t: 2610.86, text: "It found the Heppells of the Rhineland, who survived the Thirty Years' War and crossed an ocean to start over." },
  { t: 2618.56, text: "It traced the Barbeau line all the way back to a merchant's house in Pons, in Saintonge, and to the merchant's son Jean, who in 1685 left a Protestant town as a Catholic soldier of the King, crossed the ocean, and never went home." },
  { t: 2637.56, text: "Joseph Wilfred Barbeau, baptised at Saint-Édouard de Napierville in 1870, was the original anchor; he died at North Adams in 1938, before the Citizenship Act took effect, so the law looked deeper into the same family." },
  { t: 2656.2, text: "It found four others." },
  { t: 2659.24, text: "Maria Lepage, baptised Marie Hélène Cléophée at Notre-Dame-de-la-Victoire in 1883, died at North Adams in 1958." },
  { t: 2672.34, text: "Virginie Noël, baptised at Saint-Michel de Bellechasse in 1855, died in 1948." },
  { t: 2680.52, text: "Henri C. Clermont, baptised at Saint-Liboire de Bagot in 1880, died in 1953." },
  { t: 2690.66, text: "Hermenegilde Hepel, of the Rimouski Heppell line, died in 1957." },
  { t: 2697.56, text: "Four Canadian-born ancestors lived past the 1947 line, in the direct line Daniel shares with his sister Renee and their Lepel cousins, the children of Ronald and of his brother Paul." },
  { t: 2711.68, text: "The law reaches all of them equally." },
  { t: 2715.2, text: "Any one of these ancestors is enough." },
  { t: 2718.02, text: "Together they are conclusive." },
  { t: 2720.96, text: "Their family had crossed four countries, fought in or fled from a dozen wars, weathered famines and revolutions and revocations, lived under nine kings and one Kaiser, watched empires rise and fall in their neighbours' valleys, and emerged in the twenty-first century, in a New England mill town beneath the Berkshires, holding all four threads at once." },
  { t: 2748.28, text: "This is who they came from." },
  { t: 2750.4, text: "This is how they got here." },
  { t: 2752.78, text: "Daniel grew up among the Lavoies, Gagnons, and Tremblays of North Adams." },
  { t: 2757.3, text: "His father Ronald had two brothers: Paul, who served in the United States Air Force at Elmendorf Air Force Base in Alaska and later married Judy Darling; and Roger, who passed away." },
  { t: 2772.0, text: "Aunt Judy is still living, retired now in Florida." },
  { t: 2777.04, text: "Daniel's grandfather Alcide had a first cousin, René Lepel, the son of Alcide's uncle Joseph, a younger brother of Hermenegilde." },
  { t: 2787.44, text: "René became a United States naval officer, and in 1944, in the months after the liberation of southern France, he met and married Pierrette Macastar at Marseille, a young woman of Greek and French-Moroccan descent and one of the first war brides to come home to the family." },
  { t: 2807.5, text: "They settled in Rochester, New York." },
  { t: 2810.42, text: "Daniel's grandmother Rita had a sister, Theresa, who married Edward Garofano of North Adams; their daughter Linda Garofano stood as Daniel's godmother at his baptism at Notre Dame du Sacré Coeur." },
  { t: 2825.44, text: "Rita also had a brother, J. George Betourney, who married Lucille Collings in 1940; their son George Norman Betourney remains in North Adams to this day." },
  { t: 2838.74, text: "The four lines became one in 1962, but the families that married and lived around them were never countable on four fingers." },
];
